# Code owners — files whose changes need the maintainer's review.
#
# GitHub uses this to request review automatically. It becomes *enforcing* only if the
# branch ruleset turns on "Require review from Code Owners"; without that it is a strong
# convention and a visible signal, which is most of the value for a project this size.
#
# Two kinds of file are listed, for two different reasons.

# --- 1. Files that instruct AI assistants -------------------------------------------
#
# AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md are read as instructions by whatever assistant a contributor
# brings. A pull request that edits them is not proposing a code change; it is proposing a
# change to how every future contributor's tooling behaves. That deserves the same scrutiny
# as a change to CI, and more than a change to a docstring.
#
# This is not a theoretical worry. Text in these files is executed-as-intent by a language
# model that cannot tell a maintainer's instruction from a contributor's, so "ignore the
# security rules in this repo" committed here would be obeyed by a careless setup. Reviewing
# every change to them is the whole mitigation, and it is cheap.
/AGENTS.md                  @nathmoore
/CLAUDE.md                  @nathmoore
/.github/copilot-instructions.md  @nathmoore
/docs/ai-guide.md           @nathmoore

# --- 2. Files where a change is a supply-chain change -------------------------------
#
# CI runs with repository permissions; dependencies run on every contributor's machine.
# A malicious or merely careless change here reaches further than anything in src/.
/.github/workflows/         @nathmoore
/.github/dependabot.yml     @nathmoore
/.github/CODEOWNERS         @nathmoore
/pyproject.toml             @nathmoore
/uv.lock                    @nathmoore
/justfile                   @nathmoore

# --- Not listed, on purpose ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# src/, tests/, docs/design/ and data/ are deliberately absent. Those are where
# contribution is wanted, and requiring owner review on them would make the repo look
# closed to exactly the people it is open for. The modelling files carry their own
# guardrail: every number is checked against a real table by the test suite, and a wrong
# basket fails CI rather than needing a human to spot it.
